<!-- ========== EXECUTIVE SUMMARY ========== -->
<section id="executive-summary">
<h2>Executive Summary</h2>
<p class="section-intro">
Over the 26-year period from January 2000 to January 2026, Nike Inc. (NKE) delivered a cumulative return of approximately <strong style="color:var(--positive)">+1,282%</strong>, compounding at <strong style="color:var(--positive)">10.6% annually</strong> — meaningfully above the S&amp;P 500's long-run average. However, the path was far from smooth. Investors endured five distinct bear phases, including a devastating <strong style="color:var(--negative)">−68.5% drawdown</strong> from the November 2021 all-time high of $166.25 to the April 2025 trough near $52.31 — a decline that remains partially unresolved as of the dataset's end.
</p>
<p>
The stock's journey divides into three structural eras: a volatile but ultimately productive first decade (2000–2009, CAGR 11.6%), a golden decade of near-uninterrupted compounding (2010–2019, CAGR 21.6%), and a painful reversal (2020–2026, CAGR −6.7%) as the post-pandemic rally unwound. Annualized volatility of 31.2% places NKE firmly in the higher-volatility tier of large-cap consumer names, demanding a strong risk tolerance from buy-and-hold investors.
</p>
<div class="insight-box">
<strong>Bottom line:</strong> Nike was an exceptional compounder for two decades, turning a $10,000 investment in 2000 into over $138,000 by late 2021. The subsequent multi-year drawdown has tested conviction, but the 26-year track record still reflects a high-quality secular growth story — albeit one that demanded patience through significant volatility.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ========== PRICE TREND ANALYSIS ========== -->
<section id="price-trend-analysis">
<h2>Long-Term Price Trend Analysis</h2>
<p class="section-intro">The closing price history reveals five distinct structural phases, each shaped by company fundamentals, macroeconomic conditions, and investor sentiment.</p>

<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="priceChart"></canvas>
</div>

<h3>Structural Phases</h3>
<ul class="phase-list">
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">Phase 1: Post-Bubble Decline &amp; Recovery (2000–2004)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">NKE opened the millennium at $4.47 (split-adjusted) and initially plunged to an all-time low of <strong style="color:var(--negative)">$2.47</strong> in February 2000 — a −44.8% crash in under two months following an earnings warning. The stock spent over two years rebuilding, finally surpassing its January 2000 level in late 2002, then accelerating into a steady uptrend through 2004 as Nike's international expansion and direct-to-consumer strategy began paying off.</div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">Phase 2: Steady Ascent Through the Financial Crisis (2005–2009)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">Nike demonstrated unusual resilience during the Global Financial Crisis. While the stock pulled back −18.1% in 2008, this was a relatively shallow correction compared to the broader market's decline. The stock recovered quickly, with 2009 delivering +26.9%. Nike's brand strength and global diversification provided a defensive moat during the crisis.</div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">Phase 3: The Golden Decade (2010–2019)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">This was Nike's most remarkable period: a <strong style="color:var(--positive)">+604%</strong> cumulative return over ten years (21.6% CAGR). Key drivers included the digital transformation, China expansion, and the shift to direct-to-consumer sales. The stock rose from ~$13 to ~$93, with only 2016 (−16.4%) producing a material annual decline. Volatility moderated to the low 20s during most of this period.</div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">Phase 4: COVID Rally &amp; All-Time High (2020–2021)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">After a brief −33.4% COVID-driven selloff in early 2020, NKE staged a dramatic recovery, surging to the all-time high of <strong style="color:var(--positive)">$166.25 on November 5, 2021</strong>. The pandemic accelerated Nike's digital pivot; e-commerce revenue soared, and the stock nearly doubled from its pre-COVID level of ~$88 to over $166 in less than two years.</div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">Phase 5: The Great Unwind (2022–2026)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">From the November 2021 peak, NKE entered a sustained <strong style="color:var(--negative)">−68.5% drawdown</strong> — the largest in the dataset. Annual returns were −28.2% (2022), −7.4% (2023), −27.8% (2024), and −11.5% (2025 partial). Headwinds included excess inventory, increased competition from On Running and Hoka, a strategic reset under new leadership, and macro pressures on consumer discretionary spending. At $61.81 as of January 30, 2026, the stock was trading at levels last seen in 2018.</div>
  </li>
</ul>

<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="drawdownChart"></canvas>
</div>

<div class="insight-box">
<strong>Key observation:</strong> The current drawdown (−68.5%) is unprecedented in Nike's 26-year history by both depth and duration. It eclipses the early-2000 crash in severity and the 2008 pullback by an order of magnitude. This is the defining risk event for any investor evaluating NKE's long-term profile.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ========== VOLATILITY ANALYSIS ========== -->
<section id="volatility-analysis">
<h2>Volatility and Risk Assessment</h2>
<p class="section-intro">Annualized volatility of 31.2% over 26 years places NKE above many large-cap consumer staples but in line with growth-oriented consumer discretionary names. Volatility has been highly regime-dependent.</p>

<div class="two-col">
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="volatilityChart"></canvas>
  </div>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="spreadChart"></canvas>
