<h3>Volatility Regimes</h3>
<p>The 30-day rolling annualized volatility reveals distinct clusters:</p>
<ul class="phase-list">
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">2000–2002: High Volatility (40–70%)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">The early period was marked by extreme volatility, with 30-day annualized readings frequently exceeding 50%. The February 2000 earnings shock and dot-com fallout created a turbulent environment.</div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">2003–2007: Compression (15–30%)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">As Nike's growth story stabilized, volatility compressed to a more normal range. This coincided with the stock's steady ascent and improving earnings visibility.</div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">2008–2009: Crisis Spike (~50%)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">The GFC pushed 30-day volatility back above 45%, though it remained below the 2000-era extremes — evidence of Nike's relative defensiveness.</div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">2013–2019: Low &amp; Stable (15–25%)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">The golden decade featured persistently low volatility, reflecting strong earnings growth and institutional confidence. The most investable regime for risk-adjusted returns.</div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="phase-label">2020–2026: Elevated (25–45%)</span>
    <div class="phase-detail">The COVID crash, subsequent melt-up, and prolonged drawdown have kept volatility elevated. 2025 saw annual volatility of 42%, reflecting deep uncertainty about Nike's turnaround path.</div>
  </li>
</ul>

<div class="insight-box">
<strong>Risk context:</strong> Average daily High-Low spread (as % of close) has widened meaningfully in the 2022–2026 period, signaling deteriorating trading conviction. When spreads expand alongside falling prices, it typically indicates institutional repositioning rather than retail panic — a more structural headwind.
</div>
</section>

