<!-- ========== KEY TAKEAWAYS ========== -->
<section id="key-takeaways">
<h2>Key Takeaways and Investment Implications</h2>

<div class="takeaway-grid">
  <div class="takeaway-card">
    <h4>1. Proven Long-Term Compounder</h4>
    <p>A 10.6% CAGR over 26 years — through two recessions, a pandemic, and a historic drawdown — places Nike in the upper tier of consumer discretionary performers. $10,000 invested at the start of 2000 grew to roughly $138,200 by the end of the dataset.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-card">
    <h4>2. Drawdown Severity Demands Attention</h4>
    <p>The ongoing −68.5% drawdown from the 2021 high is unprecedented for a company of Nike's quality. It erased more than a decade of gains and is the longest sustained decline in the dataset. This raises legitimate questions about structural vs. cyclical headwinds.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-card">
    <h4>3. Volatility Is the Price of Returns</h4>
    <p>At 31.2% annualized volatility, NKE has required significant risk tolerance. However, volatility has been episodic rather than chronic — long stretches of low volatility (2013–2019) were punctuated by crisis spikes. The current elevated-vol regime may persist until the fundamental picture clarifies.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-card">
    <h4>4. The 2010s Were the Exception, Not the Rule</h4>
    <p>The 21.6% CAGR of the 2010s decade was driven by unique tailwinds: China growth, digital transformation, and the DTC pivot. Investors should be cautious about extrapolating that era's returns. The 2000s (11.6% CAGR) may be a more realistic baseline for forward expectations.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-card">
    <h4>5. Volume Signals Institutional Debate</h4>
    <p>The surge in trading volume during 2024–2025 shows that the institutional investor base is actively re-evaluating Nike. This is a necessary precursor to bottoming — but not sufficient on its own. Watch for volume to normalize at lower levels as a sign of stabilization.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-card">
    <h4>6. Historical Resilience in Crises</h4>
    <p>Outside of the current drawdown, Nike's crisis performance was notably strong: −18.1% in the 2008 GFC (vs. ~−50% for the S&amp;P 500) and a rapid COVID recovery. The brand's global reach and pricing power historically provided a moat during macro stress.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="insight-box">
<strong>Investor framework:</strong> Nike's 26-year track record confirms a genuine compounder, but the current drawdown introduces a regime-change question. Long-term investors with conviction in Nike's brand moat and turnaround capability may view the current price (−68.5% from ATH) as a compelling entry point. However, the depth and duration of the drawdown warrant a margin of safety approach — position sizing and risk management matter more here than at any point since 2000.
</div>
</section>

